        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Himeji Castle</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">1333 CE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Made by</span><span class="info-stat-val">Fortified by Akamatsu Norimura in 1333, remodelled by the Kuroda clan and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, rebuilt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-terumasa">Ikeda Terumasa</a> from 1601 to 1609 and extended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/honda-tadamasa">Honda Tadamasa</a> from 1617 to 1618</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Form</span><span class="info-stat-val">A hirayama (flat hilltop) castle of stone and wood with white plaster walls: a main keep 46.4 metres high with three smaller keeps, 83 buildings, three moats and a spiral approach, on a complex of 233 hectares</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where it is now</span><span class="info-stat-val">On the hill called Himeyama, 45.6 metres above sea level, in the centre of Himeji, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyogo-prefecture">Hyōgo Prefecture</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the white plaster exterior that gave it the names Hakuro-jō and Shirasagi-jō; the maze of paths to the keep; roughly 1,000 loopholes; five National Treasures; survival of the 1945 bombing and the 1995 Great Hanshin earthquake; registration as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1993</span></div>
