    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The hill was first fortified in 1333, and the castle seen today was largely built by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-terumasa">Ikeda Terumasa</a> between 1601 and 1609, a work believed to have taken 2.5 million man-days of labour.</li><li>Its gates, baileys and walls were laid out so that an approaching force would travel in a spiral, but the castle was never attacked in this manner and the system remains untested.</li><li>It was spared demolition in the Meiji Period, survived the bombing of Himeji in 1945 and the Great Hanshin earthquake of 1995, and was registered in 1993 as one of the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Sites</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The straight distance from the Hishi Gate to the main keep is only 130 metres, but the path between them is 325 metres long.</p></aside>
    </section>

